# **This file is the only header source the Cloudflare deploy ships.**
#
# `next.config.ts` also declares headers. On the Node/Docker self-host path that
# declaration is what runs and this file is ignored. On the Cloudflare build it
# is the exact opposite: `headers()` is never applied, and only this file
# reaches a visitor. Neither file is redundant and neither is authoritative on
# its own — **they must be kept in step by hand**, and a header added to one and
# not the other silently ships to only half the users.
#
# Verified on 2026-09-19 against production: `X-Frame-Options`, declared in
# `next.config.ts` but absent here, was reaching nobody.
#
# `ALLOW_INDEXING=false` has the same shape of problem and cannot be fixed here:
# this file is static and cannot read an environment variable. **Setting it does
# not de-index the Cloudflare deploy.** To pull the site out of search, change
# `public/robots.txt` and add an `X-Robots-Tag` line below — do not rely on the
# environment variable alone.
#
# ## A rule never replaces an earlier one — it adds to it
#
# Every pattern that matches the request contributes its lines, so two rules
# that both name `Cache-Control` do not fight: the values are combined, and the
# result is a policy neither rule wrote. That is why the narrower rules below
# say `! Cache-Control` before setting their own, exactly as they already do
# for `Content-Security-Policy`. **A `Cache-Control` added to a narrower rule
# without the unset line first is a bug**, and the test in
# `lib/edge-cache-headers.test.ts` fails on it.
#
# Measured, not assumed. Dropping the `! Cache-Control` from the `/og.png` rule
# and serving the build (2026-09-23, `wrangler dev` against `dist/server`) made
# that file answer
# `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate, s-maxage=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400, public, max-age=86400`
# — both policies, with the catch-all's `max-age=0` first and therefore winning.
#
# ## Why the catch-all carries both a browser policy and an edge policy
#
# Measured on the live site, 2026-09-23: every HTML page answered
# `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` — the asset layer's default, because no
# rule here set anything else — and Cloudflare returned
# `cf-cache-status: REVALIDATED` on every single request. The edge held the
# page but was forbidden to serve it without asking the Worker first, so the
# zone cache hit rate sat at 42.55% on a site where **every** page is a static
# file produced at build time and nothing is personalised. Each of those
# revalidations is a Worker invocation the free plan has to pay for, and a
# crawl burst of exactly that shape is what produced 1,316 `exceededResources`
# 503s in one hour on 2026-09-16.
#
# The two audiences want different things and `Cache-Control` can address them
# separately:
#
#   * `max-age=0, must-revalidate` is for the **browser**, and is the same
#     instruction it already receives today. A visitor still revalidates on
#     every view, so a deploy reaches them as soon as the edge has it.
#   * `s-maxage` is for the **shared cache**, which is Cloudflare. It overrides
#     `max-age` for that cache alone, so the edge answers from its own copy for
#     an hour without waking the Worker at all.
#   * `stale-while-revalidate` lets the edge keep answering from the expired
#     copy while it refreshes in the background, so the hourly refresh costs a
#     background fetch rather than a waiting visitor.
#
# **The cost of this is deploy propagation.** The edge may go on serving the
# previous build for up to the `s-maxage` below (plus however long until the
# next request triggers the background refresh). An hour is the deliberate
# ceiling on that; purge the zone cache after a deploy that has to be visible
# immediately. Do not raise it without deciding that trade again.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; connect-src 'none'; font-src 'self'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; img-src 'self' blob: data:; object-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; webrtc 'block'; worker-src 'self' blob:
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: credentialless
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate, s-maxage=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400

# The share card is fetched by every social scraper that sees a link, and it
# only changes when someone regenerates it. It was being served
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate` like the rest of `public/`. A day is short
# enough that a regenerated card propagates on its own. No `s-maxage` is needed:
# with none set, `max-age` governs the edge too.
/og.png
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# Build output is content-addressed: every filename under here carries a content
# hash (`framework-DTZGTDtF.js`, `layout.BusCenFg.css`) or sits inside a
# build-id directory that changes on every build. A new build produces new
# names, so a cached copy can never go stale — yet these were being served with
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate`, which makes the browser re-check roughly fifteen
# files on every single page view. Measured 2026-09-18 on the live site.
#
# Verified before applying: all 69 files under this prefix are hashed or
# build-scoped; the only two unhashed files, `_buildManifest.js` and
# `_ssgManifest.js`, live inside the build-id directory.
#
# `max-age=86400, must-revalidate` was the first correction and it stopped
# short. A name that can never be reused does not need a daily re-check at all,
# and on 2026-09-23 `/_next/static/chunks/index-CoyL43pJ.js` — a hashed,
# unchangeable file — still answered `cf-cache-status: MISS`. `immutable` is
# the honest statement of what these files are: a year, and no revalidation,
# for both the browser and the edge.
/_next/static/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

